<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kauaʻi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kauai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kauaʻi is the oldest and northernmost of the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaiian Islands</a>, separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a> by the wide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaieiewaho-channel">Kaʻieʻiewaho Channel</a>. Its mountainous centre, Mount Waiʻaleʻale, is one of the wettest places on earth, and Waimea Canyon cuts deeply into its western side. Ruled by its own paramount chiefs, it remained independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> until its ruler Kaʻumualiʻi accepted his overlordship by agreement in 1810.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kauai.jpg" alt="Kauaʻi" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Ulfl at German Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kauai_from_space_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A storm in 1796 and an epidemic in 1804 wrecked his plans to invade <b>Kauaʻi</b>, but in 1810 its ruler, Kaumualiʻi, accepted him as overlord, completing the unification of the islands under one king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>English has largely displaced Hawaiian on six of the seven inhabited islands, and Hawaiian Pidgin is more spoken than Hawaiian; only on Niʻihau, off <b>Kauaʻi</b>, is it still the language of daily life.</blockquote><a href="/language/hawaiian">Hawaiian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1782 Kamehameha, guardian of the war god Kūkāʻilimoku, defeated his cousin Kīwalaʻō, Kalaniʻōpuʻu’s son and successor, at Mokuʻōhai, and by 1795, with Western guns, ships and advisers, he had conquered every main island but <b>Kauaʻi</b> and Niʻihau, whose ruler accepted him as overlord in 1810.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li><li><blockquote>On his third voyage, Cook sighted Oahu and then <b>Kauai</b>, where he landed two days later.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-18">18 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kauaʻi</b> was settled early in Hawaiian history, and by around 1400 its districts were united under a single aliʻi nui, the paramount chief or mōʻī, ruling from Waimea on the south-west coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/chiefdom-of-kauai">Chiefdom of Kauai</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Kalanikūpule defeated Kaʻeokulani of <b>Kauaʻi</b> that year with the help of the British trader William Brown.</blockquote><a href="/state/chiefdom-of-oahu">Chiefdom of Oahu</a></li></ul></section>
